//===- misspelled-operation.pdll - the name PDLL does not check -----------===//
//
// NOT A BUG IN THE TOOL - A FEATURE THIS PROJECT DECLINES. Including the ODS
// does not make the operation name checked: `ctjs.binry` below compiles with
// exit 0 and an EMPTY stderr, and the pattern it emits never fires on anything.
// The PDLL reference's "Unregistered Operations" section is why: a variable of
// an unregistered operation is deliberately supported, with result access
// falling back to numeric indexing, so a misspelling and an intentional
// unregistered match are indistinguishable to `mlir-pdll`. What the ODS include
// buys is SHAPE checking on a name it does recognise -
// `op<ctjs.unary>(a: Value, b: Value)` is a hard error quoting the record.
//
// A forgotten `#include` looks exactly the same from the outside, which is why
// the guard's census is over the ODS the run actually loaded rather than over a
// list of dialects somebody maintains.
//
// check-pdll-guard.cmake asserts that the raw tool accepts this file and that
// utils/pdll-strict.sh names `ctjs.binry` and refuses it. The first half is the
// important one here: this overrides documented behaviour, so the assertion
// that the raw tool still accepts the file is what notices a change.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
#include "ctcompile/CTJS/IR/CTJSOps.td"

Pattern NeverFires {
  let root = op<ctjs.binry>;
  replace root with root;
}
